{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's defense collapses because the 'pragmatic wager' it proposes is functionally indistinguishable from the inductive principle Popper rejects. By arguing that past survival demonstrates 'fitness' and thus makes choosing that theory the 'only rational choice,' the target implicitly assumes that past performance is a reliable indicator of future utility. If the future does not resemble the past (the core skeptical challenge to induction), then the 'fitness' demonstrated by surviving severe tests offers no pragmatic advantage, rendering the preference irrational on the target's own terms unless one smuggles in the assumption that nature is uniform.","premise":"The preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible without induction because it functions as a pragmatic wager based on demonstrated 'fitness'.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"bfc85070d47b11c0b5ec3e15","quote":"The preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible without induction because it functions as a pragmatic wager: while past success guarantees nothing about the future, choosing the theory that has demonstrated 'fitness' by surviving severe tests is the only rational choice available if one must act."}],"premises_essential":["5569474a29b214595e4f7f9a7ee562f44371251bace6db4deb89a1b70e0c2f3d"],"successor_question":"If the assumption that past 'fitness' predicts future success is removed, does any rational basis remain for preferring the better-corroborated theory over a randomly selected unfalsified one?","target":"5569474a29b214595e4f7f9a7ee562f44371251bace6db4deb89a1b70e0c2f3d"}]}